"This surah encompasses eighteen obligatory duties. According to what Abu Hayyan recorded, the students of the famous al-Kindi, the first philosopher of the Arabs, said to him, 'O philosopher, compose something for us equal to this Quran.' He replied, 'Very well, I will not produce an equivalent for all of it, but I will make something similar to a part of it,' and worked for days. Then he came forth and said: 'By Allah, neither we nor anyone else has the power to do this. I opened the Mushaf, and Surah al-Ma'idah appeared before me; I looked, and it enjoined fidelity (keeping one's word), prohibited backsliding, made a general analysis, then made an exception, and subsequently gave news of His power and wisdom, fitting all of this into two lines. No one could express this without writing volumes.'\n\n'Uqud is the plural of 'aqd. 'Aqd means a documented contract; it is likened to a bond and knot that firmly ties one thing to another. In the dictionary, it means to bind tightly and to knot. Transferred from this meaning, a person's adoption of something, or binding oneself or another by compelling the other to silence, is called an 'aqd (contract/covenant). 'I'tiqad' (creed/conviction) also derives from this. Not every contract relies on offer and acceptance; in some, an agreement is formed merely through consent. Vows and oaths regarding the future are of this kind. 'Ahd' (covenant), on the other hand, means preserving and watching over something from state to state; documents required to be thus observed are called 'ahd'. Although 'ahd' and 'aqd' appear synonymous, 'aqd' conveys a greater sense of consolidation. 'Wafa' and 'ifa' mean fulfilling the requirements of the covenant and contract. Here, 'al-'uqud' encompasses all contracts: the legal obligations Allah has imposed upon His servants, the vows and oaths servants bind themselves to of their own accord, trusts and transactions made among people, and even treaties made with the people of war (harb), protected non-Muslim subjects (dhimmah), and others. From this, the following legal principle is deduced: The presumption in contracts is validity, unless evidence to annul them has been established.\n\nO you who believe, fulfill all the contracts to which you are bound. Faith (iman) is a contract; by this contract, you entered into covenants with Allah. Then, you make other contracts and bind yourselves of your own accord, among yourselves, or among people. Fulfill, then, all these contracts. In short, this is the root of the religion, the verdict of faith, and the command of Allah. In harmony with the name of the surah, the explanation begins first with the means of livelihood: Various animals have been made lawful for you, except those whose unlawfulness is recited or will follow below. You cannot consider hunting lawful while you are in the state of ihram; however, you may eat from animals slaughtered by those who are not in ihram. This lawfulness and unlawfulness are divine covenants. Being able to eat the meat of an animal slaughtered by someone else is the fruit of a contract between you, such as purchase or gift. The game that is forbidden while in ihram is land game, not sea game.\n\n'Bahimah' means any animal that lacks intellect. What is made lawful here is not animals (baha'im) in an absolute sense, but four-legged animals living on land and in the sea. 'An'am' refers to domestic livestock such as camels, cattle, sheep, and goats. Solid-hoofed animals such as horses, mules, and donkeys are not included in an'am. The compound 'bahimat al-an'am' either functions as an explicative genitive (idafat bayaniyyah) meaning 'the animals known as an'am'—in which case game animals such as gazelles and deer also become lawful—or as a comparative genitive (idafat lamiyyah tashbihiyyah) meaning 'animals that are like an'am', rendering wild animals that resemble livestock in chewing the cud and lacking canine teeth lawful. Under either interpretation, bahimat al-an'am does not include marine animals.\n\nThe following have been made unlawful for you: 1. Maytah (carrion, that is, what dies without being slaughtered). 2. Blood (spilled blood is impure and unlawful). 3. Swineflesh (the pig itself is impure). 4. That over which the name of other than Allah has been invoked at the time of slaughter (for instance, that slaughtered in the name of al-Lat and al-'Uzza). These are fundamental. Those following them fall religiously under their scope. During the Jahiliyyah period, although some did not eat carrion, they ate the following, which are essentially of the nature of carrion: 5. The suffocated (that which died from suffocation in any way). 6. The beaten (that which died from a blow). 7. The fallen (that which died by falling from a height). 8. The gored (that which was gored or butted). 9. That partially eaten by a wild beast (that which was destroyed by a predatory animal). Since the blood of these five was not drained by slaughter, they are entirely carrion and unlawful.\n\nAllah has forbidden carrion, blood, swineflesh, animals slaughtered in the name of other than Allah, the suffocated, the beaten, the fallen from above, the gored, and animals partially eaten by wild beasts. However, if these animals are reached before dying and are properly slaughtered according to the prescribed rules, they become lawful. Quran commentators have put forward differing opinions regarding the scope of this exception. While some stated that the exception applies only to that partially eaten by a wild beast, the majority stated that it encompasses all of them, from the suffocated to that partially eaten by a wild beast. In any event, this exception does not encompass things that cannot be slaughtered, such as carrion, blood, and swine.\n\nIn Islam, slaughter carried out according to the prescribed rules is called 'tazkiyah'. Tazkiyah is terminating the animal's life by slitting its throat and draining its blood. The place of slaughter is from the base of the neck to beneath the jaw. There are four vessels that must be cut: the windpipe, the esophagus, and the two jugular veins. According to the Hanafi school, at least three of these must be severed. The slaughter instrument must be sharp, and the animal must not be tormented. It is a condition that the slaughterer be a Muslim or from the People of the Book and pronounce the Basmalah.\n\nAnimals slaughtered on stone altars (nusub) are also unlawful. During the Jahiliyyah period, slaughters were performed for idols or in front of them. In addition, seeking fortunes with divining arrows (azlam)—that is, fortune-telling and gambling—has also been prohibited. All these prohibitions are characterized as departing from obedience to Allah and transgression (fisq). Believers must strictly stay away from them.\n\nThese verses were revealed during the Farewell Pilgrimage on the day of Arafah at the time of Friday prayer: 'Today I have perfected for you your religion, completed My favor upon you, and chosen for you Islam as your religion.' With this verse, it was announced that the religion was completed and that the possibility of abrogation (naskh) no longer remained. The Companions rejoiced greatly at this verse; however, Abu Bakr wept, understanding that the death of the Messenger of Allah was drawing near.\n\nNonetheless, in a state of dire necessity (the danger of dying of hunger), a person may eat from forbidden things, provided they do not transgress the limits and do not usurp another's right. Allah is Forgiving, Merciful. As stated in the preceding verses, this exception is valid only for the moment of necessity.\n\nWhen the Companions asked what had been made lawful for them, the Messenger of Allah was commanded to proclaim: 'Lawful to you are good and wholesome things. Eat of what the hunting animals you have trained as Allah taught you catch for you, and mention the name of Allah over it.' Furthermore, the food of the People of the Book is lawful for you, and your food is lawful for them. Chaste women among the believing women and chaste women from among those who were given the Scripture before you are lawful for you, provided that you pay their dowries in an honorable manner. Whoever rejects faith, their deeds come to naught, and in the Hereafter they will be among the losers.\n\nAccording to what Ka'b narrated, Gabriel (peace be upon him) would not enter a house in which there was a dog. Thereupon, the Prophet (peace be upon him) ordered the killing of dogs and assigned Abu Rafi' to this task. When the dogs in Medina were killed, the Companions came and asked, 'What has been made lawful for us?' The Messenger of Allah remained silent, and then this ayah was revealed. In another narration, when 'Adi ibn Hatim and Zayd al-Khayl asked about hunting with dogs and falcons, this verse was revealed once again. Although different narrations indicate that the ayah was revealed before or after the verse of prohibition, both cases are consistent with the context of the discourse.\n\nIn this ayah, 'tayyibat' (wholesome things) were made lawful. Tayyibat are clean and good things that sound human nature finds pleasant. The taste of those with bad habits is not taken into account. The original principle regarding things is permissibility, but khaba'ith (foul things) are excluded from this generality. This is a major principle in determining the lawful and the unlawful. While the pre-Islamic Arabs of Jahiliyyah ate filthy things such as carrion, they would arbitrarily forbid certain animals to themselves out of mere delusion. By declaring that wholesome things are lawful, this verse abolished those customs.\n\nJawarih means predatory hunting animals. Mukallib means training these animals for hunting. If a trained dog or bird of prey wounds and kills the prey, and the animal is already dead by the time the hunter reaches it, wounding takes the place of slaughtering and the game becomes lawful. However, if the animal eats from the prey it killed, this means it caught it for itself, and it is not eaten. It must be released while pronouncing the Basmalah. There are different opinions on this matter; according to the Hanafi school, the fact that a bird of prey ate from it does not preclude its lawfulness.\n\nThe food of the People of the Book is lawful for you, and your food is lawful for them. The word 'ta'am' (food) has generally been interpreted as slaughtered meat, because regarding foods like bread, there is no difference between the People of the Book and others; the difference lies in slaughtered meat and game. What the People of the Book slaughter is eaten as long as they do not slaughter it in the name of anyone other than Allah. However, if you hear that they slaughtered it in the name of the Messiah, it is not permissible to eat it. Furthermore, it is lawful for you to marry chaste, free women from among the People of the Book by giving them their dowry. As for Muslim women marrying men from the People of the Book, it is never permissible.\n\nIn the continuation of the ayah comes the command of wudu (ablution). When you rise for prayer, wash your faces and your hands up to the elbows, wipe your heads, and wash your feet up to the ankles. If you are in a state of major ritual impurity (junub), purify yourselves. If you cannot find water, perform tayammum (dry ablution). This verse was revealed when water could not be found during the expedition in which Aisha's necklace was lost. There is disagreement regarding whether the feet should be washed or wiped due to a difference in recitation. The majority considers washing to be obligatory, and the Sunnah also supports this. Wiping is performed over leather socks.\n\nO you who believe! When you rise for prayer—that is, when you intend to perform prayer—wash your faces and your hands up to the elbows, wipe your heads, and wash your feet up to the ankles. Prayer is not valid without this purification. This is called 'tuhur' (purification) or 'wudu' (ablution). The obligatory acts of ablution are four: three washings and one wiping. Imam al-Shafi'i also considered intention and proper order to be obligatory, making them six; Ahmad ibn Hanbal added rinsing the inside of the mouth and nose as well, stating there are eight obligations. Among the Hanafis, intention, order, rinsing the mouth, and sniffing water into the nose are Sunnah. Although the outward wording of the verse might seem to require a separate ablution for every prayer, the majority does not hold this view. Our Prophet performed five daily prayers with a single ablution, which demonstrated its permissibility. Furthermore, there is consensus that the ayah should be understood as 'when you rise for prayer while you do not have ablution.' In wiping the head, the letter 'ba' is used; from this, the Hanafis deduced the meaning of passing the hand over the head, holding that the obligatory amount of wiping is one-fourth of the head, whereas Malik and Ahmad ibn Hanbal deemed the entire head obligatory, and al-Shafi'i considered the touch of a single finger sufficient.\n\nWashing means letting water flow completely over the limb. Rubbing is not a condition. Ablution is purification from hadath (ritual impurity); it is different from purification from najasah (physical filth). In ablution, physical impurity must be removed beforehand. Washing once is sufficient for fulfilling the obligation, while washing twice or three times is Sunnah. Wiping, on the other hand, means touching something and passing the hand over it; its obligatory element is contact. Wiping the head is the touching of the wet hand to the required amount. Repeating the wipe is not Sunnah, though the hair may be smoothed down gently. If you are in a state of janabah—that is, if seminal fluid has been emitted or sexual intercourse has taken place—you must perform ghusl and wash thoroughly from head to toe. Rinsing the mouth and sniffing water into the nose, which are Sunnah in ablution, are obligatory in ghusl.\n\nIf you are ill, on a journey, have relieved yourselves, or have touched women, and you cannot find water, then perform tayammum with clean earth. Make your intention and wipe over your faces and forearms. Tayammum takes the place of wudu or ghusl when water cannot be found or cannot be used. In the verse, it is stipulated that 'sa'id' (the earth) be clean; something must be wiped from the hands onto the face and arms. While according to the Hanafis merely touching the earth with the hand is sufficient, according to the Shafi'is something must definitely be transferred from the hand to the face and arms. The debate over whether tayammum is permissible on stone, marble, and minerals originates from this point.\n\nWith these commands of purification, Allah does not intend to place difficulty upon you, but rather He intends to purify you from material and spiritual filth and complete His favor upon you, so that you may be grateful. There is no hardship in religion; the wisdom behind ablution, ghusl, and tayammum is both physical and spiritual purification. Gratitude increases blessing, and the increase of blessing becomes a cause for gratitude. Here, the completion of favor is an indication of the blessings of the Hereafter alongside the blessings of this world. The greatest blessing is the consciousness of gratitude. Those who attain the pleasure of Allah are those who give thanks.\n\nRemember Allah's favor upon you and His covenant (mithaq) that He made with you. This covenant is the moment you said, 'We hear and we obey.' It is the primordial covenant established at the creation of Adam and encompasses the obedience and pledge of allegiance of all believers to the Prophet. Mujahid linked this covenant to the statement 'Am I not your Lord?' in verse 172 of Surah al-A'raf, whereas al-Suddi linked it to rational and legal evidences. In short, do not forget the pledge of servanthood, give thanks for the blessing of Islam, and fulfill your covenants. Allah knows the secrets within your breasts; therefore, give importance to both outward and inward purification.\n\nO you who believe! Be steadfast for Allah as witnesses to justice. And let not the hatred of a people drive you to injustice. Justice is nearer to righteousness (taqwa). Fear Allah; indeed, Allah is well aware of what you do. Allah has promised those who believe and do righteous deeds forgiveness and a great reward. As for those who disbelieve and deny the verses, they are the companions of the Hellfire. This ayah, unlike its counterpart in Surah an-Nisa, emphasizes observing justice even in the state of hostility. There, domestic affairs come to the fore, whereas here, foreign relations come to prominence. Believers should rely on Allah alone.\n\nRemember Allah's favor: when a people intended to stretch out their hands against you, but Allah withheld their hands from you. This refers to the assassination attempt by the Jews of Banu Nadir against our Prophet, or generally the thwarting of the attacks of the polytheists. Believers should place their trust in Allah (tawakkul) and rely on nothing else. The consequence of breaching agreements is evil; the example of the Children of Israel points to this. Allah had taken a covenant from them and sent twelve overseers (naqib) from among them. It was promised that if they established prayer, gave zakah, believed in the messengers, supported them, and lent a goodly loan in the way of Allah, their sins would be expiated and they would be admitted into gardens of Paradise.\n\nYet the Children of Israel broke their pledge; therefore Allah cursed them and hardened their hearts. They would distort words from their contexts and forgot a portion of what they had been reminded of. Except for a few among them, treachery is constantly witnessed from them. Even so, pardon them and overlook [their misdeeds], for Allah loves those who do good. Those who said, \"We are Christians,\" also forgot much of what they had been reminded of, and enmity and hatred were cast among them until the Day of Resurrection. Allah will inform them of what they used to do.\n\nBecause the Children of Israel broke the covenant, Allah cursed them and hardened their hearts. They displaced words from their contexts and altered the Torah. They forgot a significant portion of what they had been reminded of. They commit acts of treachery against our Prophet, though those among them who believe are an exception. Allah commanded forgiveness and overlooking; this is a general pardon, showing that not every act of treachery must be punished. The subsequent verses on warfare did not abrogate this pardon, but merely clarified it.\n\nA pledge had also been taken from the Christians, but they too forgot a significant portion of the reminders given to them, especially abandoning tawhid (monotheism) and belief in the final Prophet. Thereupon, Allah cast enmity and hatred among them until the Day of Resurrection. They made a craft of the evils they committed, and they will receive the recompense for it in the Hereafter.\n\nO People of the Book! Our Messenger Muhammad has come to you, clarifying much of what you used to conceal of the Scripture and overlooking much. There has come to you from Allah a light and a clear Book (the Quran). Through this Book, Allah guides those who seek His pleasure to the ways of peace and brings them out of darkness into light. This address invites the Jews and Christians to the right path and exposes the truths they had concealed.\n\nThose who said, \"Allah is the Messiah, son of Mary,\" have surely disbelieved. Yet if Allah wished to destroy the Messiah, his mother, and everyone on the earth, no one could prevent Him. To Allah belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth; He creates whatever He wills. The Jews and the Christians said, \"We are the children of Allah and His beloved ones.\" Say: Why then does He punish you for your sins? Rather, you are human beings among those He has created.\n\nOur Messenger has come to you after an interval between the messengers, lest you should say, \"No bearer of good tidings and no warner has come to us.\" Now, a bearer of good tidings and a warner has indeed come to you. And Allah is capable of all things.\n\nThe Christians' assertion that \"Allah is the Messiah\" stems from their doctrine of the Trinity. Although some commentators have stated that this verse was revealed concerning specific sects (such as the Jacobites, Chaldeans, etc.), this belief is generally an innovation (bid'ah) embraced by the majority of Christians. The disputes over whether there is one nature or two natures in the Messiah also reflect various aspects of this deviation.\n\nFollowing the Council of Nicaea, official Christian doctrine took shape, Arius and his followers were excommunicated, and the Trinity was accepted. This council obscured the truth and left an enigma of misguidance for subsequent generations. In reality, the Spirit of Truth (Paraclete) foretold in earlier scriptures arrived with Muhammad (peace be upon him), but the Christians denied it.\n\nIn his commentary on verse 72 of Surah al-Ma'idah, Elmalılı examines the major denominations of Christianity and their understanding of the Trinity. He notes that there are three major sects—namely the Melkites (Catholic and Orthodox), Nestorianism, and Jacobitism (Monophysite)—and that among them, the Melkites are the most widespread. He summarizes the emergence of these sects from the earliest councils onward, along with the condemnation of movements such as Arianism, Macedonianism, and Nestorianism.\n\nNestorianism contends that there are two persons (divine and human) in Jesus, and that what was born of Mary was solely human. By contrast, Jacobitism (Monophysites) claims that there is only a single nature (divine) in Jesus and that the divine swallowed up the human. The historical spread of these sects and their conflicts with one another are recounted, with particular emphasis on the Jacobite presence in Egypt, Abyssinia, and Syria.\n\nProtestantism, on the other hand, emerged later, opposing the Pope and accepting the Holy Bible as the sole authority. Yet Protestants do not differ from Catholics regarding the Trinity and the divinity of the Messiah. The differences between them center on points such as prioritizing faith over works, permitting clerical marriage, and vigorously adopting the doctrine of predestination. Elmalılı considers it a contradiction that Protestants claim to value reason while blindly accepting irrational dogmas such as the Trinity.\n\nIn conclusion, all Christian sects (except for the Arians) accept the doctrine of the Trinity and the divinity of the Messiah. However, they disagree on whether a human nature exists in the Messiah alongside the divine nature. While the Monophysites say, \"The Messiah is Allah,\" the others converge on saying, \"Allah is the Messiah, son of Mary.\" Thus, the verse rejects this latter statement, declaring that those who say, \"Allah is the Messiah, son of Mary,\" are disbelievers.\n\nIn the continuation of the verse, it is emphasized that if Allah willed to destroy the Messiah, his mother, and all those on earth, no one could prevent it. Proceeding from this, the transience and helplessness of the Messiah and other beings are demonstrated. The Divine, however, is exalted above all transience and possesses absolute power. For this reason, neither the Messiah nor any other being can be identified with Allah or become a partner to Him. With this explanation, Elmalılı refutes both the Christian claim of the Trinity and pantheistic concepts such as wahdat al-wujud (unity of existence).\n\nAllah creates as He wills; His creation is not in the manner of breaking off a part from Himself or emanation (sudur). No being separates from Him, is born of Him, or originates from Him. The creation of Jesus without a mother is also an example of this power and does not indicate his divinity. If Allah wills, He creates from an origin, and if He wills, without an origin. Just as He created Adam from dust, Eve from Adam, and Jesus from Mary, every creation occurs by His own will.\n\nMiracles such as Jesus creating a bird with his hands and raising the dead do not demonstrate his divinity, but rather Allah's power. Had Allah not willed it, Jesus could not have done these things. Allah is capable of all things; created beings do not diminish His power in the least, but are rather His handiwork. All beings are subject to Allah; their power is relative, while true power belongs to Allah. Therefore, those who say, \"Allah is the Messiah, son of Mary,\" are disbelievers.\n\nThe Jews and the Christians considered themselves privileged, saying, \"We are the children of Allah and His beloved ones.\" According to Ibn Abbas, the original reason for this statement was the response given by the Jews to the call of the Prophet (peace be upon him). The Christians, on the other hand, meant their affiliation with the Messiah. Even if this expression was initially used as a metaphor, it was later adopted as a literal truth, and at the Council of Nicaea, the view of begottenness was reinforced with the phrase \"from the essence of the Father.\" Through this claim, they deemed themselves exempt from accountability and aimed to establish dominion over others.\n\nIn response to them, Allah stated: \"You are merely human beings like other people; you are His servants. Allah punishes you for your sins; He forgives whom He wills and punishes whom He wills. Whatever you may be, you cannot escape being created beings.\" Allah's dominion encompasses everything in the heavens and the earth; no creature holds a special standing before Him. Everyone will arrive in His presence and be held to account.\n\nO People of the Book! Our Messenger Muhammad (peace be upon him) has come to you during a period of cessation (fatrah) when the succession of messengers had ceased. He explains the truth to you so that in the Hereafter you may not say, \"No bearer of good tidings or warner came to us.\" Allah has power over all things. Believe in this Prophet and obey him; attain the forgiveness of your sins. Today, you cannot deny the coming of this Prophet, whose arrival was foretold in the Torah and the Gospel.\n\nMoses (Musa) said to his people: \"Remember Allah's favor upon you when He raised prophets from among you, made you kings (or free people), and gave you what He had not given to anyone else in the worlds.\" Foremost among these blessings was their liberation from bondage in Egypt and their attainment of freedom. According to some exegetes, the expression \"kings\" means \"free people.\" This freedom signifies the possession of personal and political rights.\n\nMoses continued: \"O my people! Enter the holy land which Allah has destined for you, and do not turn back, or you will turn about as losers.\" But the Children of Israel said: \"There is a tyrannical people there; we will never enter it until they depart from it.\" Two men who feared Allah said: \"Enter upon them through the gate, and you will prevail; and put your trust in Allah.\" But the people disobeyed, saying: \"Go, you and your Lord, and fight; we are sitting right here.\" Thereupon Moses prayed: \"My Lord! I have power over none except myself and my brother; so separate us from this defiantly disobedient (fasiq) people.\" And Allah decreed: \"Then indeed, that holy land is forbidden to them for forty years; they shall wander bewildered through the earth.\"\n\nThe \"Holy Land\" (al-Ard al-Muqaddasah) refers to Jerusalem and the blessed territories surrounding it. Although there are various narrations concerning the boundaries of this place, al-Tabari states that the most accurate approach is simply to suffice with saying the \"holy land.\" However, this region does not fall outside the area between the Euphrates and al-Arish.\n\nWhen the people of Moses were commanded to enter the holy land promised to them, they rejected this command, saying that there was a tyrannical and mighty people there. They said, \"We will never enter it until they leave it,\" making this condition an impossibility. This was an act of defiance against Allah and the prophet, and was akin to an excuse of the kind \"until a camel goes through the eye of a needle.\"\n\nIn connection with this event, the exegetes recount what happened to the twelve spies sent by the Children of Israel. Having seen that the tyrants were enormous and formidable, these spies became terrified, and upon their return, they spread the news and discouraged the people from fighting. Only Joshua, the brave companion of Moses, and Caleb, his son-in-law, trusted in Allah and stated that the task would be easy.\n\nThe story of \"Og, son of Anak\" (Uj ibn Unuq) is narrated as a fable describing the might of the tyrannical people. In this fable, it is told that Og was a giant three thousand three hundred and thirty-three cubits tall and three thousand years old. However, such accounts are in reality allegories symbolizing the collapse of a state. The Quran summarizes these legends with the expression \"a tyrannical people\" and teaches how to distinguish truth from fantasy.\n\nIn the face of the people's stubbornness, Moses prayed: \"O my Lord! I have power over none except myself and my brother; so separate us from this defiantly disobedient people.\" Because of this rebellion, Allah forbade the holy land to them for forty years; throughout this period, they would wander bewildered and landless in the Desert of Tih. This was a punishment for those who abused their freedom.\n\nNarrations differ as to whether Moses and Aaron remained in Tih throughout the forty years. Some say that they did not suffer torment in Tih, but rather educated the Children of Israel and raised a new generation. According to the widespread narration, Moses and Aaron passed away in Tih, and subsequently Joshua became a prophet and conquered the holy land along with the new generation.\n\nFollowing this narrative, the story of the two sons of Adam (Cain and Abel), which corresponds to the state of the Children of Israel, is recounted. Each of them offered a sacrifice; one was accepted, while the other was not. The one whose sacrifice was not accepted swore to kill his brother. The other said, \"Allah only accepts from those who fear Him,\" adding that he would not kill him, but rather preferred that his brother bear his sin along with his own and end up in Hell.\n\nYielding to his own desires (nafs), Cain killed his brother and became one of the losers. While he did not know what to do with the corpse, Allah sent a raven scratching the ground to show him how to bury it. Cain was filled with regret. This story emphasizes the evil of envy and murder.\n\nIt was because of this incident that the following decree was ordained for the Children of Israel: whoever kills a soul—unless for a soul or for corruption done in the land—it is as if he had killed all of mankind; and whoever saves a life, it is as if he had saved all of mankind. This illustrates the sanctity of human life and the gravity of the sin of unlawful killing.\n\nIn this section, the link between the preceding narrative and the decrees given to the Children of Israel is addressed. Some scholars (such as al-Hasan and al-Dahhak) thought that the recounted murder took place among the Children of Israel and that the rulings were therefore specific to them. However, the majority holds that the story took place between the two sons of Adam, and that these decrees were revealed because of the universal message conveyed by the narrative. This message is that murder is an enormous crime and that emotions such as envy and selfishness drag man into catastrophe. It was precisely because this disposition was prominent among the Children of Israel that more severe rulings (retaliation [qisas] and capital punishment) were made obligatory for them.\n\nThe penalty for those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger and strive to spread corruption throughout the land is determined, according to the gravity of the crime, as execution, crucifixion, the cutting off of hands and feet on opposite sides, or exile. Various narrations are reported regarding the occasion of revelation of this verse, foremost among them the story of the people of Uraynah. The majority of jurists state that this ruling applies to highway robbers and encompasses both the disbeliever and the transgressing (fasiq) Muslim. The expression \"waging war against Allah\" is metaphorical; it signifies disobeying the commands of Allah or attacking the believers.\n\nIt is disputed whether the particle \"or\" among the penalties indicates an option or a distribution based on the offense. The majority (jumhur) maintains that the punishments are graded according to the severity of the crime and that the judge does not have the discretion to choose. However, the possibility of a choice aimed at mitigating the punishment cannot be entirely dismissed. Indeed, Imam al-Shafi'i interpreted the penalty of exile in two ways: continuous pursuit for those who have not been caught, and imprisonment for those who merely made threats. Imam al-A'zam (Abu Hanifah), on the other hand, understood exile as imprisonment and stated that banishing someone to another land is objectionable.\n\nIn the continuation of the verse, it is stated that those who repent before they are captured are exempt from the punishment. However, personal rights (retaliation, compensation for property) are not dropped; victims or their heirs may pursue their claims. This repentance drops the right of Allah, but does not drop the rights of fellow humans. Then, in the 35th verse, the believers are commanded to have God-consciousness (taqwa), seek a means of approach (wasilah) to Allah, and engage in jihad. A means of approach refers to every kind of righteous deed and intention that draws one closer to Allah; this is attained through supererogatory acts of worship, good character, and striving for Allah's pleasure. Seeking a means of approach is an element that complements faith and piety.\n\nIn verses 36–37, it is explained that even if the disbelievers wished to offer everything on earth as a ransom to be saved from the punishment on the Day of Resurrection, it would not be accepted from them, and they will remain in eternal torment. This serves as a warning for the believers. In verses 38–39, the ruling of amputating the hand is established as the punishment for theft. Theft is defined as taking someone else's property in secret, and for the penalty to be applied, the property must reach a specific value and be taken from a secure place of custody. Glad tidings are given that those who repent and mend their ways will be forgiven.\n\nElmalılı details the necessary conditions for the execution of the penalty for theft. He states that for the act of theft to incur the prescribed legal punishment (hadd), the property must reach a specific threshold (nisab) and be kept in a secured place (hirz). He relates the views of different schools of jurisprudence: while the Zahiris and Kharijites impose the punishment for any theft, whether small or great, the Hanafis stipulate the conditions of nisab and hirz. In addition, he draws attention to the principle that prescribed penalties are averted by doubts; circumstances such as doubt regarding permissibility (shubhat al-ibahah), doubt regarding entitlement (shubhat al-haqq), and doubt arising from dire necessity (shubhat al-idtirar) waive the hadd. Umar's suspension of the penalty for theft during the year of famine is cited as an example of this application of doubt.\n\nHe explains that there are differing juridical opinions regarding the amount of nisab, such as a quarter of a dinar, three dirhams, or ten dirhams. While Imam al-A'zam (Abu Hanifah) stipulates ten dirhams, al-Shafi'i considers a quarter of a dinar sufficient. Elmalılı argues that the threshold of nisab may change over time, maintaining that this amount can be updated in light of price differences between gold and silver and to eliminate any doubt arising from necessity. He emphasizes that the penalty is not merely retribution, but also a deterrent punishment (nakal) from Allah; amputating the thief's hand purifies both the individual and society from corruption.\n\nIn the commentary on verse 39, it is stated that Allah is forgiving if the thief repents. It is debated whether the punishment is dropped if repentance occurs before the sentence is executed. The majority (the Hanafis) hold that it is not dropped unless the owner of the stolen property forgives, whereas al-Shafi'i maintains that it is dropped. Drawing from the phrase 'and amends his conduct,' Elmalılı explains that showing good conduct after repentance and reimbursing the stolen property are necessary; however, if the penalty of amputation has already been carried out, financial restitution is not required.\n\nIn verse 40, it is recalled that the sovereignty of the heavens and the earth belongs to Allah, and that He punishes whom He wills and forgives whom He wills. In verse 41, addressing the Prophet, it is stated that those who race into disbelief (the hypocrites and the Jews) should not grieve him. Their characteristics are listed: they listen eagerly to falsehood, distort words from their proper contexts, and listen on behalf of another group who have not come to you. If Allah intends to put someone to trial (fitnah), no one can avail them against Allah in the least. For them is disgrace in this world, and for them in the Hereafter is a tremendous punishment.\n\nAs the occasion of revelation, it is recounted that the Jews distorted the punishment of stoning (rajm) and appealed to the Prophet. Although stoning was commanded in the Torah for adulterers, Jewish scholars altered this punishment, mitigating it to forty lashes and blackening the face. The Prophet uncovered the truth by administering an oath to Abdullah ibn Suriya and executed the sentence of stoning. Furthermore, the disparity in blood money (diyah) between the Banu Nadir and Banu Qurayzah is also among the reasons for the revelation of these verses.\n\nIn the commentary on verses 41–43, the habit of the Jews and hypocrites of listening to falsehood and consuming illicit gain (suht) is addressed. Suht encompasses all kinds of unlawful earnings, such as bribery, the wages of prostitution, and the price of wine. It is conveyed that the Prophet is free to judge between them or abstain, but if he judges, he must judge with justice. There are differing opinions as to whether this discretion was abrogated: while some say it is obligatory for a Muslim judge to adjudicate between non-Muslims who apply to him, others maintain that he remains free to choose. Ultimately, it is emphasized that those who do not judge by what Allah has revealed are the disbelievers, the wrongdoers, or the defiantly disobedient (fasiqun).\n\nAllah Almighty criticizes the Jews for seeking your arbitration while they possess the Torah, and then turning away from the judgment delivered. This conduct of theirs shows that they do not truly believe in the Torah or in Allah's judgment. For had they truly believed, they would have been content with the ruling found in their own scripture. In truth, they merely follow their desires. Therefore, you should not grieve over their disbelief.\n\nThe Torah was revealed as guidance and light. The prophets and their successors—the rabbaniyyun (godly scholars devoted to knowledge and worship) and the ahbar (Jewish scholars)—judged by this scripture. A rabbani is one who nurtures people with knowledge, educating them first with fundamental matters. The ahbar are prominent scholars who write down and record knowledge. They are charged with preserving the Book of Allah. This preservation takes place both by feeling it in their hearts and teaching it with their tongues, and by abiding by its rulings and putting them into practice. Allah commands, 'Do not fear the people, but fear Me, and do not sell My verses for a small price.' Whoever does not judge by what Allah has revealed—it is they who are the disbelievers.\n\nThe rulings of legal retribution (qisas) are prescribed in the Torah: a life for a life, an eye for an eye, a nose for a nose, an ear for an ear, a tooth for a tooth, and for wounds is legal retribution. Qisas is founded on the principle of exact equivalence and equality, such as a life for a life or an eye for an eye. However, in cases of wounds where equivalence is not possible, qisas is not applied; instead, arsh (stipulated compensation) or hukumah al-'adl (equitable indemnification assessed by experts) is paid. Retribution is primarily a human right (haqq al-'abd); the wronged party may forgive. Forgiving serves as an expiation for that person.\n\n'There is life for you in legal retribution, O people of understanding!' (Al-Baqarah 2:179). Retribution was ordained not to kill, but to protect life. Executing a murderer means saving an innocent life. Under this powerful manifestation of justice, forgiving is an even greater virtue. 'Whoever saves a life, it is as if he had saved all of mankind.' (Al-Ma'idah 5:32). Thus, this ruling is a guidance and a light, comprising justice on one side and benevolence on the other.\n\nLet the people of the Gospel also judge by what Allah has revealed. Jesus was sent as a prophet confirming the Torah, and the Gospel was given to him. In the Gospel too, there is guidance and light. Whoever does not judge by what Allah has revealed—it is they who are the defiantly disobedient (fasiqun). Disbelief (kufr) arises from rejecting the divine ruling; wrongdoing (zulm) from placing a matter where it does not belong; and defiance (fisq) from departing from the truth. These three descriptions vary according to the degree and nature of turning away from the ruling.\n\nAnd unto you (O Muhammad!) We revealed the Book in truth, confirming the scriptures that came before it and as a guardian (muhaymin) over them. Muhaymin signifies one who watches over, protects, bears witness, and is trustworthy. The Quran preserves the true rulings in the Torah and the Gospel and rectifies those that were corrupted. From now on, no ruling that does not receive its confirmation can be acted upon. So judge between them by what Allah has revealed, and do not follow their desires. Unto each community We have appointed a law (shir'ah, a specific path) and an open way (minhaj, a clear method). Shir'ah consists of secondary rulings that vary according to times and circumstances, whereas minhaj consists of immutable, primary principles. The universal foundations shared by all prophets constitute the minhaj, while the rulings specific to each of them constitute the shir'ah.\n\nHad Allah willed, He would have made you a single community. However, He willed to test you through the different legal paths He gave you. So race toward good deeds. Unto Allah is the return of you all, and He will inform you concerning that wherein you used to differ.\n\nIn verse 49, our Prophet (peace be upon him) is commanded to judge by the true Book revealed by Allah and not to follow their desires. According to a narration, the Jews came seeking a ruling in their own favor in order to lure our Prophet into discord, but this verse was revealed. Here, attention is drawn to the fact that those who do not accept Allah's judgment commit a grave, hidden sin. Furthermore, it is emphasized that this sin will certainly be punished in this world, and that judges must judge with truth and justice.\n\nIn verse 50, those who abandon the judgment of Allah and seek the judgment of Jahiliyyah (pre-Islamic ignorance) are condemned. The judgment of Jahiliyyah entails evils such as injustice, sycophancy, malice, and inequality. Yet, there is no judgment better than the judgment of Allah. It is narrated that this verse was revealed concerning a murder incident between the Banu Nadir and the Banu Qurayzah, where our Prophet judged with equality, but the Banu Nadir were dissatisfied with this and demanded the custom of Jahiliyyah.\n\nIn verses 51-53, the believers are commanded not to take Jews and Christians as allies. They are allies of one another; whoever takes them as allies is one of them. The hypocrites, in whose hearts is a disease, rush among them out of fear of a misfortune. According to a narration, 'Abdullah ibn Ubayy declared that he would not sever his alliance with the Jews, whereupon these verses were revealed. In the end, it is announced that the deeds of these hypocrites will come to naught.\n\nIn verses 54-56, it is stated that whoever apostatizes from their religion, Allah will replace them with a people whom He loves and who love Him, humble toward the believers, and dignified and firm against the disbelievers. This community strives in the cause of Allah and does not fear the blame of any blamer. This is the grace of Allah. In the commentary of the verse, various views are transmitted regarding who this community might be: such as Abu Bakr and his companions, the people of Yemen, or the Persians. Furthermore, it is emphasized that the true ally is only Allah, His Messenger, and the believers.\n\nIn verses 57-63, it is commanded not to take as allies those among the People of the Book and the disbelievers who treat religion as a matter of ridicule and amusement. When they are called to prayer, they mock it. Our Prophet is directed to ask the People of the Book whether their resentment toward them is solely because they believe in Allah and what has been revealed. Then, those who incurred Allah's curse and wrath, and were turned into apes and pigs, and those who worshiped false deities (taghut) are described. The hypocrites' claims of faith are refuted, and it is stated that most of the People of the Book race with one another in sin, hostility, and consuming illicit gain. Their rabbis and scholars ought to have forbidden them.\n\nIn verses 64-66, the statement of the Jews that 'Allah's hand is tied' is condemned. On the contrary, Allah's hands are outstretched. Enmity and hatred have been cast among them until the Day of Resurrection. Had the People of the Book believed and been mindful of Allah, their sins would have been pardoned and they would have been admitted into the gardens of bliss. Had they observed the Torah and the Gospel, they would have eaten of blessings from above them and from beneath their feet. Among them is a moderate group, but most of them do evil.\n\nIn verses 67-69, our Prophet is commanded to convey what has been revealed to him; otherwise, he will not have fulfilled his prophetic mission. Allah will protect him from the people. 'O People of the Book! You have no standing until you observe the Torah, the Gospel, and what has been revealed to you.' Then, whoever among the believers, the Jews, the Sabians, and the Christians believes in Allah and the Last Day and performs righteous deeds, there will be no fear upon them, nor will they grieve.\n\nIn verses 70-75, it is recalled that a covenant was taken from the Children of Israel and messengers were sent to them, but they denied some and killed others. They thought there would be no trial, so they grew blind and deaf. Those who said, 'Allah is the Messiah, son of Mary,' and 'Allah is the third of three,' have certainly disbelieved. Yet the Messiah had commanded them to worship Allah. Let them repent and seek His forgiveness. Jesus, son of Mary, is only a messenger; his mother was a woman of truth; they both used to eat food. Despite the verses being made clear to them, they still turn away.\n\nHere, it is explained that if a nation fails to appreciate the blessing of Islam, Allah will replace it with another nation. Historically, the Arabs, then the Persians, and then the Turks undertook this service. If the Turks are also ungrateful, another community will take their place. Allah will send many more communities until the Day of Resurrection.\n\nIn verse 55, it is stated that the protector and ally (wali) of the believers is only Allah, His Messenger, and the believers. Those believers who establish prayer and give zakah. There are various narrations concerning the occasion of revelation of this verse: according to one narration, Ali gave his ring to a beggar while in bowing (ruku'), whereupon this verse was revealed. However, this does not mean that the verse is exclusive to Ali; it encompasses all believers in general.\n\nIn verses 56-58, it is emphasized that the party of Allah (Hizbullah) will be triumphant, and that those who ridicule religion must not be taken as allies. In particular, the disbelievers who mock the call to prayer (the adhan) must not be taken as allies. Concerning the occasion of revelation of these verses, it is related that a Christian who mocked the adhan perished in a fire.\n\nIn verses 59-60, a reply is given to the People of the Book: 'Do you resent us only because we believe in Allah and what has been revealed to us?' Then, mention is made of those whom Allah cursed, who incurred His wrath, who were transformed into apes and pigs, and who worshiped false deities (taghut). This announces the state of those who are in an even worse condition than the People of the Book. 'Maskh' (metamorphosis) may be spiritual, or it may encompass both physical form and moral character.\n\nIn verses 61-63, the condition of the hypocrites is described: they say 'We believe,' but they enter as disbelievers and leave as such. Most of them race with one another in sin, transgression, and consuming illicit gain. While the rabbis (rabbaniyyun) and scholars should have forbidden them, they do not forbid them, and this is indeed a vile practice. The most severe rebuke is found in this verse.\n\nIn verse 64, the statement of the Jews, 'Allah's hand is tied,' is cited. This implies that Allah is stingy or powerless. Yet both of Allah's hands are outstretched; He bestows as He wills. This utterance is the Jews attributing their own stinginess to Allah. The phrase in the verse, 'May their hands be tied and may they be cursed for what they said,' is either an invocation against them or an announcement of its consequence.\n\nThis statement of the Jews is based on a flawed conception regarding Allah's power and will. Yet Allah is absolute in power and supreme in generosity. The word 'hand' is used figuratively in language; it does not denote a physical hand. While the early scholars (Salaf) accepted such allegorical verses (mutashabihat) without interpretation, later theologians (mutakallimun) provided rational interpretations (ta'wil). In our own language, the idiom 'open-handed' is used to express generosity.\n\nThe word 'yad' (hand) attributed to Allah the Exalted carries several meanings in Arabic: power and might, possession, assistance, and special designation. As in the saying 'One hand is above another,' the meaning of power is widely used. When it is said, 'This land is in so-and-so's hand (yad),' ownership is intended. The expression 'I made this with my own hand' conveys that special care and attention were devoted to it. According to most scholars, the word 'yad' used in reference to Allah signifies power or favor. Abu al-Hasan al-Ash'ari, however, regards it as an attribute subsisting in the Divine Essence, distinct from power. Nevertheless, it must be remembered that 'yad' here is a metaphorical reply to the Jews' claim that 'Allah's hand is tied.' The dual expression 'two hands' serves emphasis and intensification; if we wish, it may also be interpreted as one being an open hand (mercy) and the other a closed hand (wrath).\n\nThe Jews attributed tight-fistedness to Allah by saying, \"Allah's hand is tied.\" The Quran refutes this statement of theirs and proclaims that both of Allah's hands are outstretched, bestowing as He wills. Because of their stubbornness, this disbelief of theirs will only increase their transgression. Allah has cast enmity and hatred among them until the Day of Resurrection. Whenever they kindle a fire for war, Allah extinguishes it. He does not love those who spread corruption and He punishes them. Yet, if they believe and guard against evil, He will expiate their sins and admit them into the Gardens of Delight.\n\nIf the People of the Book had properly observed the Torah, the Gospel, and what was sent down to them from their Lord, they would have eaten from above them and from beneath their feet—that is, they would have attained abundance from every direction. There are seven different interpretations of this expression: general abundance, rain from the sky and produce from the earth, fruits of the trees and cultivated crops, unearned grants and blessings earned through labor, public benefits provided by the state and private enterprise, the happiness of Muslims at the moment of victory, and the depiction of the blessings of the Hereafter. However, they did not do this. Among them is a moderate (muqtasid) community, but they are few. The majority of them commit corruption.\n\nThere are two views regarding the moderate community (ummah muqtasidah): The first is that they are those who believed from among the People of the Book, such as 'Abdullah ibn Salam and the Negus (Najashi). The second is that they are those who, although they did not believe, are not obstinate, but rather moderate and impartial. The preferred view is the second one. Our Prophet was commanded to convey all that was revealed to him, and it was made known that Allah would protect him from the people. According to narration, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) stated upon this command that his fear had completely vanished. It was requested that the People of the Book be told: \"You stand on nothing until you observe the Torah, the Gospel, and all that was revealed to you.\"\n\nIn the noble ayah, the believers, the Jews, the Sabians, and the Christians are mentioned, and it is stated: \"Whoever truly believes in Allah and the Last Day and works righteousness, no fear shall come upon them, nor shall they grieve.\" This indicates that all these groups will attain salvation on the condition of faith. However, this cannot occur through merely outward faith; true faith and righteous deeds are required together. The ayah also serves as a warning to believers: being outwardly Muslim is not sufficient for salvation. Furthermore, in terms of worldly rulings, there is equality between Muslims and non-Muslims, but salvation in the Hereafter depends upon true faith.\n\nSome orientalists have claimed that there is abrogation (naskh) between ayah 62 of Surah al-Baqarah and ayah 69 of Surah al-Ma'idah. This is not correct. Between the two ayahs there is only a difference in grammatical inflection (i'rab); the meaning and ruling are identical. Both ayahs invite the People of the Book and other groups to faith and promise salvation to those who believe. The interpretations of French orientalists on this matter are politically motivated and aimed at misrepresenting Islam's concept of tolerance. Muslim jurists (mujtahids) did not say that these ayahs were abrogated; on the contrary, they stated that they corroborate each other.\n\nIn this section, Elmalılı primarily emphasizes that no Islamic scholar, whether classical or contemporary, has stated or thought that the two ayahs in Surah al-Ma'idah (ayah 69 and the accompanying verses) are abrogated. He notes that the fundamentals of faith and creed—for example, the principle that those who believe in Allah and the Last Day and perform righteous deeds will experience neither fear nor grief—are an eternal truth, and that no religion can be a true religion without accepting this principle. Abrogation is possible only in subsidiary rulings (furu'), that is, in practical matters that change according to time and place; abrogation is not permissible in such fundamental tenets of belief.\n\nElmalılı characterizes the claim by some Western (particularly French) writers—that Muslim jurists abrogated the first of the ayahs in question with the second—as an outright slander. He states that this claim is the product of both ignorance and malicious intent. For abrogation requires the presence of two contradictory rulings; however, in these ayahs the ruling is identical, with a difference only in the naming of the enumerated groups. Furthermore, he adds that Muslim scholars are in consensus on this matter, whereas those who cast slander upon them are mired in political bigotry.\n\nThe author explains that the religion of Islam contains both constant (usul) and variable (furu') rulings. He states that the verbal and practical Sunnah of the Prophet (peace be upon him) also encompasses rulings that vary according to circumstances, but that these variable rulings are not contrary to fundamental principles. He stresses that the two ayahs in Surah al-Ma'idah belong to the constant principles and cannot be interpreted according to environmental conditions. Furthermore, he indicates that these ayahs must be evaluated together with the preceding and succeeding verses; otherwise, it will lead to misunderstandings.\n\nElmalılı provides the etymology of the word 'Sabi'in' (Sabians). He explains that it derives from the root 's-b-'' in Arabic, meaning one who departs from one religion into another. He touches upon the variations in recitation (qira'ah), noting that Nafi' and Abu Ja'far recited it without a hamzah. He explains that the literal meaning of the word is 'one who departs from a well-known religion to another,' which is why 'Sabi'in' refers to those outside the religions of Islam, Judaism, and Christianity. In addition, he adds that the word is the name of a specific religion or sect, whose adherents are called 'Sabi'i' or 'Sabi'.\n\nHe conveys various opinions regarding who the Sabians were: According to some, they were a group who claimed adherence to the religion of the prophets Noah or Enoch (Idris), but over time drifted toward star worship and idolatry. He notes that Sabianism was influenced by ancient Syriac and Chaldean traditions, and that during the Abbasid era, several scholars emerged from this group. He describes their acts of worship as including seven daily prayers, thirty days of fasting, pilgrimage, and festivals, noting that in terms of intention and reverence (khushu'), their prayer resembled the Muslim prayer.\n\nLastly, Elmalılı sets forth the fundamental distinction between Hanifism (the monotheistic faith of Tawhid) and Sabianism. He stresses that the Hanifs did not accept the mediation of either spiritual or corporeal beings to draw near to Allah, worshipping Allah directly. The Sabians, on the other hand, in an effort to take spiritual entities as intermediaries, eventually deviated into the worship of stars and idols, thereby falling into polytheism (shirk). Through this comparison, he demonstrates Islam's pure creed of Tawhid and its superiority over other faiths.\n\nIn describing the belief system of the Sabians, Elmalılı first conveys their views concerning spiritual beings (ruhaniyyun). According to them, the Creator of the universe is Allah, but He cannot be reached directly; rather, there are spiritual beings who serve as intermediaries. These spiritual beings, like angels, are incorporeal entities that obey Allah's command, and humans hope to attain closeness to Allah by drawing near to them.\n\nThe influence of these spiritual beings has three aspects: in terms of essence (jawhar), action (fi'l), and state (hal). In their essence, they are free from matter; in their action, they act as intermediaries in creation and governance; and in their state, they are in perpetual remembrance (dhikr) of Allah and worship. It is believed that these spiritual beings hold influence over the stars, celestial bodies, and natural phenomena.\n\nThe Sabians were divided into various groups around this belief: Ashab al-Hayakil (worshipers of statues), Ashab al-Ashkhas (worshipers of concrete idols), the Hizbaniyyah (a pantheistic group maintaining that Allah manifests in human individuals), and the Hiribbaniyyah (a more moderate group remaining devoted to the prophets). Each group adopted different practices as a method of reaching spiritual beings.\n\nThe Hanifs, on the other hand, opposed these beliefs. Prophets such as Abraham (peace be upon him) counseled worshiping Allah alone and associating no partners with Him. Elmalılı summarizes the view of the Hanifs as follows: Spiritual beings and the stars are proofs of Allah's power; one must not worship them, but turn toward their Creator. Man's elevation is possible only through monotheism (tawhid).\n\nWhether the Sabians are People of the Book is disputed. Imam al-A'zam considered certain Sabians (those who followed Yahya ibn Zakariyya) to be People of the Book, stating that what they slaughter may be eaten and their women may be married. However, the Imamayn (Abu Yusuf and Muhammad) and other scholars stated that the Sabians who worship stars are polytheists (mushrikun) and cannot be considered People of the Book. Elmalılı emphasizes that mentioning the Sabians separately from the Jews and Christians in the Quran indicates that they are not People of the Book.\n\nIn his commentary on verse 69 of Surah al-Ma'idah, Elmalılı draws attention to the expression 'Sabi'in' mentioned in the verse. According to him, the Johannites (Christians of Saint John), one of the Christian sects, are also included in the 'Sabi'in' group, and therefore the 'Sabi'in' in the verse refers to a branch of the Christians. At this point, he criticizes some translators for treating the 'Sabi'in' as a separate group and states that this leads to misunderstandings. Emphasizing that Islam is the highest degree of Hanifism, Elmalılı states that everyone must accept this religion.\n\nThe essence of the 69th verse is this: Whosoever believes in Allah and the Last Day and performs righteous deeds—whether Muslim, Jew, Christian, or Sabian—there shall be no fear upon them, nor shall they grieve. This ruling is universal and always valid. Faith is to accept everything coming from Allah, while faith in the hereafter is to affirm the Day of Reckoning. Righteous deeds consist of obeying Allah's commands and prohibitions, doing good, and abstaining from evil. Whoever possesses these conditions attains salvation.\n\nIn verses 70–71, the covenant taken from the Children of Israel is recalled. Allah sent them prophets, but whenever they encountered commands that were difficult for their desires, they denied some of the prophets and killed others. They thought that no affliction would befall them, so they became blind and deaf. Then they repented, and Allah accepted their repentance. Yet again, most of them became blind and deaf. This shows that they did not fulfill the condition of faith and righteous deeds.\n\nIn verses 72–73, the beliefs of the Christians are criticized. Those who say, 'Allah is the Messiah, the son of Mary,' have disbelieved. Yet the Messiah called the Children of Israel to worship Allah alone and forbade polytheism (shirk). Those who say, 'Allah is the third of three,' have also certainly disbelieved. Elmalılı explains that in terms of Arabic grammar, this expression means 'the third of three,' not 'one of three.' The Christian belief in the Trinity consists of accepting three deities and considering Allah the third among them, which is blatant polytheism.\n\nIn verse 74, it is reported that there is a painful punishment for those who do not renounce these words and repent. As for Allah, He is Most Forgiving and Most Merciful (Ghafur and Rahim); He forgives those who repent. In verse 75, it is emphasized that the Messiah was merely a prophet, before whom many prophets had passed away. His mother Mary was a woman of truth (siddiqah). They both ate food—that is, they had human needs. This is clear proof that they could not be deities. Moreover, the fact that the Messiah had peers and counterparts shows that he was not a deity, but a prophet.\n\nIn verse 76, the meaninglessness of worshiping things besides Allah that can neither benefit nor harm them is expressed. Allah hears and knows all things. Helpless beings, however, can never be deities. This verse warns both Christians and idolaters.\n\nAllah warns the People of the Book against unjustly exceeding the limits in their religion. Excess in religion is of two kinds: Justified excess is investigating the truths of the religion and meticulously implementing its rulings, which is praised. Unjust excess, on the other hand, is exceeding the limits of truth by deviating into falsehood. The Christians fell into excess by deifying the Messiah, while the Jews did so by denying his prophethood. At the foundation of this extremism lies the blind imitation of predecessors and following the desires of the soul. The distinguishing feature between faith and disbelief is the difference between servitude to Allah, Who is the Truth, and servitude to the desire of the soul.\n\nThose who disbelieved from among the Children of Israel were cursed by the tongue of David and Jesus, the son of Mary. This curse was because of their rebellion and their transgressions against the truth. David's curse was directed at those who violated the Sabbath prohibition, while Jesus's curse was directed at those who were ungrateful after the miracle of the heavenly table. They did not prevent one another from the evil they committed; how vile was what they used to do. Many of the People of the Book take the disbelievers as allies. Allah has become angry with them and has prepared an eternal punishment for them. If they had believed in Allah, the Prophet, and what was revealed, they would not have taken the disbelievers as allies; but most of them have transgressed.\n\nThe most severe of people in animosity toward the believers are the Jews and the polytheists. The hostility of the Jews is greater because of their worldly ambition, hardness of heart, and stubbornness against the Truth. In contrast, those who say, 'We are Christians,' are closer in affection to the believers. The reason for this is that among them are priests and monks, and they are not arrogant. Priests occupy themselves with knowledge and worship, while monks renounce the world out of fear of the hereafter. These qualities make them more inclined to accept the truth.\n\nWhen they hear the Quran revealed to the Prophet, the eyes of the Christians who realize that it is the truth overflow with tears, and they say, 'O our Lord, we have believed, so record us among the witnesses.' They hope that Allah will admit them into Paradise along with the righteous. Because of these sincere words, Allah rewards them with gardens beneath which rivers flow. Those who disbelieve and deny the verses are the companions of Hellfire. The Negus and his companions are among those who were the occasion for the revelation of these verses.\n\nAddressing the believers, it is commanded that they not declare forbidden the good things that Allah has made lawful, and that they not exceed the limits. Allah does not love those who exceed the limits. They must eat from the lawful and pure provisions and fear Allah. They are not held accountable for unintentional oaths, but there is an expiation for breaking deliberate oaths: feeding ten poor persons, or clothing them, or freeing a slave; whoever is unable to afford this must fast for three days. They are asked to guard their oaths and be grateful for Allah's verses.\n\nDo not consider unlawful the good and wholesome things that Allah has made lawful for you. Do not abandon what is lawful by going to extremes like past nations. According to narration, a group of Companions, deeply moved by the Prophet's warnings concerning the Day of Judgment, gathered at the house of Uthman ibn Maz'un and decided to fast continuously, abstain from meat and fat, stay away from women, and renounce the world. When the Prophet heard about this, he told them that their own selves had a right over them, that he himself prayed and slept, fasted and broke his fast, ate meat and fat, and approached women, saying: 'Whoever turns away from my Sunnah is not of me.' It was upon this incident that these verses were revealed. A believer loves what is pleasant and wholesome, yet lives in accordance with Allah's command.\n\nThe command not to consider what is lawful as unlawful gives rise to a dilemma regarding oaths: If a person swears an oath to make something lawful forbidden to himself, if he breaks his oath, he acts contrary to the command 'fulfill your contracts'; if he keeps his oath, he acts contrary to the command 'do not consider lawful things as unlawful.' To resolve this problem, Allah Almighty made known that oaths are divided into two categories: unintentional oaths (laghw, sworn vainly and not regarded as a contract) and binding oaths (mun'aqidah, made deliberately and regarded as a contract). We are not held accountable for unintentional oaths, but binding oaths are considered contracts, and if broken, expiation (kaffarah) is required. The expiation is to feed ten poor persons for a day, or to clothe them, or to free a slave; whoever lacks the means must fast for three days. There are differing views between Imam Abu Hanifah and Imam al-Shafi'i regarding the exact amount of expiation. If breaking the oath is more beneficial, it is permissible to break it by offering expiation.\n\nIn the noble verse, intoxicants, gambling, altars/idols (erected stones), and divining arrows are characterized as an abomination of Satan's handiwork, and avoiding them is commanded. Satan desires only to sow enmity and hatred among you through intoxicants and gambling, and to avert you from the remembrance of Allah and from prayer. Therefore, obey Allah and obey the Prophet, and beware of evil. But if you turn away, know that the duty of the Prophet is only to deliver the message clearly. This verse is the third and final verse regarding the prohibition of intoxicants. In the preceding verses, it had been stated that intoxicants are a grave sin and that one must not approach prayer while intoxicated.\n\nAfter the verse prohibiting intoxicants was revealed, the Companions asked about the condition of those who had passed away previously having consumed intoxicants. Thereupon, the following verse was revealed: There is no sin upon those who believe and perform righteous deeds for what they consumed in the past, so long as they fear Allah, remain steadfast in faith, continue doing righteous deeds, then again fear Allah and do good. In this verse, piety (taqwa) is mentioned in three degrees, ultimately reaching the station of spiritual excellence (ihsan). This signifies taqwa across the past, present, and future; the relationship of a person with their own self, with fellow people, and with Allah; and the initial, intermediate, and final stages.\n\nAllah Almighty made known that He would test the believers by forbidding hunting while in the state of ihram. In the year of Hudaybiyyah, game animals approached so close to the believers that they could be caught by hand or spear. This was a test to distinguish those who fear Allah from those who do not. Whoever hunts despite this prohibition must pay an expiation by sending a sacrificial animal equivalent to the game they killed to the Kaaba, or by feeding the poor, or by fasting. Marine game, however, is lawful for one in ihram. Allah made the Kaaba, the sacred months, the sacrificial offering, and the garlanded animals a means of order for humankind. These are so that you may know that Allah knows all things. The duty of the Prophet is only to deliver the message; Allah knows what you reveal and what you conceal. Say: The bad and the good are not equal, even though the abundance of the bad may impress you. Fear Allah, O people of understanding, so that you may attain salvation.\n\nO you who believe! Do not kill game while you are in the state of ihram. Whoever hunts and kills deliberately—that is, knowing that they are in ihram and that hunting is forbidden—must slaughter an equivalent sacrificial animal (camel, cattle, or sheep/goat) as a penalty for the game killed. This equivalence is determined by the appraisal of two just persons. The sacrificial animal is sent to the Kaaba. If one chooses, it is also permissible to feed the poor or to fast in lieu of this penalty. This penalty offers options so that the offender may taste the consequence of their deed. Those who hunt forgetfully or by mistake are also subject to this penalty; this is the opinion of many scholars. According to the Hanafi and Maliki schools, the hunted animal is treated as carrion and cannot be eaten; al-Shafi'i, however, states that if it was slaughtered, it may be eaten.\n\nThe game of the sea and its food have been made lawful for you, both while in ihram and during normal times. Both travelers and non-travelers may benefit from it. Land game, however, is forbidden to you as long as you are in the state of ihram. There is no harm in eating what has been hunted by someone not in ihram, provided you did not signal or command it to be hunted. Seafood, fish, and the like are always lawful. Relying on the hadith 'Its water is pure and its dead are lawful,' al-Shafi'i asserts that all marine animals are lawful, whereas the majority restrict this to fish. Even fish that die naturally in the sea are not eaten. This verse was revealed concerning the Banu Mudlij, who inquired about fish stranded on the shore. The word 'sea' (bahr) also encompasses large bodies of water such as rivers and lakes. In short, land hunting is forbidden in ihram and within the Sacred Precinct (Haram), while sea hunting is lawful under all circumstances. Fear Allah, and remember that unto Him you will be gathered.\n\nAllah made the Kaaba, the sacred month, the sacrificial offerings, and the garlands means of sustaining the religious and worldly affairs of humankind. These demonstrate that Allah is All-Knowing and All-Wise. Even if we cannot fully comprehend the underlying wisdom of these acts of worship, we must perform them as commanded. Allah's punishment is severe, yet He is also Forgiving and Merciful. Therefore, we should both fear Him and hope for His mercy.\n\nThe duty of the Prophet is only to convey the message; and he has fulfilled it. You no longer have any right to make excuses or demand other things from him. Say: The foul and the pure, the evil and the good are not equal, even though the abundance of the evil might impress you. People of understanding should prefer what is pure and good, not what is merely plentiful. Lawful earning, even if little, is far better than that which is plentiful yet forbidden. Those who associate partners with Allah consider legitimate their invented practices such as bahirah, sa'ibah, wasilah, and ham; whereas Allah never commanded these. They merely imitate their forefathers. When it is said to them, 'Come to what Allah has revealed and to the Prophet,' they say, 'We follow the way of our forefathers.' Yet their forefathers possessed no knowledge and were not guided.\n\nO you who believe! Take care of yourselves. If you are rightly guided, those who go astray cannot harm you. This does not mean that everyone should only look out for their own concerns; on the contrary, enjoining good and forbidding evil is a requirement of this verse. First, a person must rectify oneself, and then influence society. Narrated from Abu Bakr: If people see an evil and do not change it, Allah will afflict them all with punishment. In the end, to Allah you will all return, and you will receive the recompense for what you used to do.\n\nWitnessing at the time of making a bequest (wasiyyah) is important. Two just persons from among you, or if death approaches while traveling, two non-Muslims may act as witnesses. If you suspect them, you detain them after the prayer and make them swear by Allah: \"We will not sell our oath for any price, nor will we conceal the testimony of Allah; otherwise, we would surely be sinners.\" If it becomes apparent that they were lying, two others from the deceased's next of kin step forward and swear an oath truer than the oath of the former ones. This regulation ensures that witnesses tell the truth. Fear Allah; He does not guide the defiantly disobedient.\n\nIn this section, it is explained that if a Muslim cannot find witnesses while making a bequest on a journey, he may take two people from among non-Muslims (the People of the Book or others) as witnesses. Scholars such as Ibn Abbas, Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, and Mujahid hold this view. However, this testimony is permissible only when traveling abroad and when no Muslim witness can be found. Abu Musa encountered such a case in Kufa and accepted their testimony after administering an oath to the witnesses.\n\nIt is disputed whether the ruling of this verse was abrogated (naskh) by the debt verse in Surah al-Baqarah (2/282). Some scholars state that because the debt verse stipulates that witnesses must be Muslim, this verse is abrogated (mansukh). Fakhr al-Din al-Razi, however, rejects this abrogation with six proofs: the expression 'others from outside your group' in the verse, the stipulation of travel, the administering of oaths, the reason for revelation (sabab al-nuzul), the consensus of the Companions, and the principle of necessity (darurah). According to him, the testimony of non-believers is permissible in cases of necessity, and this ruling remains valid.\n\nIf suspicion arises that the witnesses are lying, they are made to take an oath. According to most mujtahids, administering an oath to a witness is not legitimate, yet this verse shows that it is permissible in cases of suspicion. If the treachery of the witnesses becomes apparent, two individuals from the party whose rights were violated are brought forward in their place and take an oath. This ruling also applies to executors who carry out the will; once their breach of trust is established, they are dismissed from their duty.\n\nIn verse 108, it is stated that administering an oath in this manner is more likely to ensure that witnesses provide proper testimony or fear the consequences of lying. In verse 109, it is reported that on the Day Allah gathers the messengers, He will ask them how their communities responded, and the messengers will reply, 'We have no knowledge; surely You are the Knower of the unseen.' This recalls the great reckoning in the Hereafter and warns and instills fear in the believers.\n\nIn verses 110–111, the blessings bestowed upon Jesus are enumerated: being supported with Gabriel, speaking in the cradle and in maturity, being taught the Book and wisdom, fashioning a bird out of clay and breathing life into it, healing the blind and the leper, raising the dead, and being protected from the Children of Israel. It is also recounted that it was revealed to the disciples to believe, and they said, 'We have believed, and bear witness that we are Muslims.' This points to the miracles of Jesus and the sincerity of the disciples.\n\nIn verses 112–115, the disciples ask, 'Can your Lord send down to us a table spread with food from heaven?' Jesus warns them, 'Fear Allah, if you are believers.' The disciples explain their purpose: they wish to eat from it, to have their hearts reassured, and to witness to its truth. Jesus prays, 'O Allah, send down to us a table from heaven to be a recurring feast for us, for the first of us and the last of us, and a sign from You.' Allah announces that He will send it down, but whoever disbelieves afterward will be subjected to an unprecedented punishment. Commentators note that there are differing narrations as to whether this table actually descended or not.\n\nIn the final section of Surah al-Ma'idah, various narrations concerning the request of the people of Jesus for the table to be sent down are related. According to the first narration, upon the prayer of Jesus, a red table spread descended containing food such as fish, vegetables, and bread. Jesus pleaded with Allah that this bounty might be a mercy. When some of those who ate from the table later rebelled, they were transformed into apes and swine. Christians made the Sunday on which this event occurred a feast day.\n\nIn the second narration, the table descended every other day for forty days, and whenever the poor and the sick ate from it, they grew wealthy or were healed. However, when Allah commanded that the table be designated for the poor rather than the rich, the people fell into dispute, and eighty-three individuals were transformed into apes and swine. According to a third narration, when the people realized the danger of such a miracle and sought forgiveness (istighfar), the table did not descend.\n\nQadi al-Baydawi, after citing these narrations, states that some Sufis interpreted the table as the realities of knowledge. According to him, this story points to the danger of an individual making a request unsuited to their spiritual station. The table of Jesus is an instructive lesson when compared to the table of Islam granted to Muslims: the greater the blessing, the more severe the punishment for disbelief.\n\nFollowing these reminders come the verses in which Allah questions Jesus: 'Did you say to the people, \"Take me and my mother as two deities besides Allah\"?' Jesus declares that he said no such thing and that he commanded them only to worship Allah. He explains that he was a witness over them as long as he remained among them, but after Allah caused him to die (took him up), Allah alone was the Watcher over them.\n\nThe response of Jesus is an exemplary model of total submission and proper etiquette (adab). He glorifies Allah and affirms that he would never utter words unbefitting of him. Bowing before Allah's absolute will, he says, 'If You punish them, they are indeed Your servants; and if You forgive them, You indeed are the Exalted in Might, the Wise.' These verses refute the Christian doctrine of the Trinity and emphasize that Jesus is merely a servant.\n\nAfter this questioning, Allah gives glad tidings of the Day when the truthful will benefit from their truthfulness. On that Day, they will abide eternally in gardens beneath which rivers flow; Allah will be pleased with them, and they will be pleased with Him. That is the supreme triumph. To Allah belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth, and He is capable of all things.\n\nThe conclusion of the surah calls the believers to Allah's pleasure and to steadfast truthfulness. No blessing in this world is as precious as Allah's pleasure. Muslims must not break their covenants as the Jews and Christians did, but must uphold uprightness solely for the pleasure of Allah. Surah al-Ma'idah concludes with the words 'Praise be to Allah' and continues with Surah al-An'am."